GL paint: keep the memory surface and the upload texture across frames.

Once a window has a GL context, every paint of it goes through the GL
path: what cairo draws lands in a memory surface (patch 0002), the parts of
that surface the paint touched are uploaded as textures at the end, and
the back buffer is swapped.  Two things in that path were done afresh for
every frame, and both grow with the window:

- The memory surface was allocated per paint, sized to the paint's clip
  box.  For a browser window that is several megabytes -- above the size
  a C library serves from its heap -- so each frame mapped fresh pages,
  faulted every one of them in as cairo cleared and drew, and unmapped
  them at the end, with the invalidation of every other processor's
  translations that an unmap carries.  A page continuously repainting
  (an animation, a scroll) paid that per frame, in proportion to its
  area, on the toolkit's main thread.  The context now holds one surface
  the size of the window and hands it out for every frame; the device
  offset places the clip box in it exactly as a surface cut to size would,
  and nothing outside the region a paint clears and draws is ever read.

- gdk_gl_texture_from_surface's generic upload defined a new GL texture
  for every rectangle of every frame, filled it, drew it and destroyed
  it.  On a device where defining a texture is a round trip to the host
  and an allocation and a release in the kernel, the frame's time went
  there.  The paint context now keeps one texture the size of the window
  (device pixels); each rectangle is uploaded into its own place with
  glTexSubImage2D and drawn from there, all rectangles in one draw.  The
  texture-rectangle variant (legacy contexts without NPOT textures)
  keeps the old per-rectangle code.

Upstream: GTK 3 does the same per-frame work on Wayland and is not
going to change; GTK 4 keeps its buffers.  LikeOS-specific in that the
costs above are what they are on this kernel and this GPU.

